WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.583
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Cat-Out has decided

00:00:01.583 --> 00:00:03.270
to set up their
packaging subscriber

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:06.120
as a serverless containerized
app on Cloud Run.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.590
Keep watching to find out how.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:14.110
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:17.140
In the last episode, we learned
about the different options

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.020
and authentication methods
that Cloud Pub/Sub supports

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.287
for push message delivery.

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:22.370
[JAZZY INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:26.350
We also setup Cloud Functions
as the push subscriber endpoint.

00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.020
Today, let's set up Cat-Out's
packaging subscriber

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:32.049
application on serverless
platform Cloud Run.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:35.380
But before we do that, Cloud
Run and Cloud Functions

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:36.610
are both serverless.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.750
So when should you use
one over the other?

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.040
Well, Cloud Functions
are great for inventor

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.090
of an applications.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:45.340
You deploy code as
functions like call them

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.000
via HTTP request, or set it to
trigger based on a background

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:49.760
event.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.910
But it is possible
that for some services

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:54.850
you need more control over
the execution environment

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.310
and the language, or
you're already running

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.810
the service in a container.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:01.120
In such cases, you
would want to set up

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.840
the service on Cloud Run, which
enables you to package code

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.240
in a stateless container, then
invoke it via HTTP requests.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.670
So today, let's help Cat-Out
set up packaging service

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:15.640
as a push subscriber
endpoint on Cloud Run.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.410
To get setup, log into
the Google Cloud console.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.860
Select your project
and open Cloud Shell.

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.740
If you've not already played
with Python docs samples,

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.190
then clone the app
repository using git clone.

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:30.640
Then cd into the folder we
just downloaded, and go to run,

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.020
and then pubsub Then,
launch the editor

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.100
so we can see the code
for our application.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.910
In the Explorer
on the left, find

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:42.520
the folder for Python samples
and go to run and pubsub.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.380
Let's see what's going on here.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.240
Main.py has the server set up
that handles incoming request.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.240
Then, there's the handler that
processes the pubsub message

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.860
and logs a greeting,
hello world.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:58.220
And it's also returning
a tool for response code.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.520
Remember from the last
episode, the subscriber

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.630
needs to respond back with
success codes such as 200, 204,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.600
et cetera, because it
acts as an acknowledgment

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.759
for the complete
processing of the message.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:15.580
And if we return HTTP error
codes such as 400 or 500,

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.940
that indicates that the
message will be retried.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.560
And, of course,
the Docker file--

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.550
that defines the operating
environment for the service.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:27.130
The contents of the
Docker file will obviously

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.210
vary by the language
of your choice.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.960
Now, in order to push
this code to Cloud Run,

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.170
we need to build a container,
push it to Container Registry,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.280
and then deploy it.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.680
So let's head on over
to our Cloud Shell,

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.710
and we will use gcloud
builds submit with tag

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.430
gcr.io/projectname/servicename.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.150
We will see a success with the
source and the image address.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.950
Now we're ready to deploy, and
for that we execute the command

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.030
gcloud run deploy, the
name of the service,

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.660
which in this case
is packaging-service,

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:00.700
and provide the image
address, which is

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.660
gcr.io/projectid/container-name.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:08.050
For this example, I'm choosing
Cloud Run fully managed option,

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:10.420
but you can select Anthos, too.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.380
Select the region.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:13.180
I picked US central, here.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:16.360
We have to say no to the
allow authenticator prompt

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:20.110
because we only want Pub/Sub to
access the packaging service.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.650
Once it is deployed, we
have the service URL.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:26.380
When we click on it, it
leads us to forbidden.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.390
This is expected
since we said no

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.340
to the unauthenticated front.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:32.910
At this point, we have
the packaging service.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:36.070
Now, how do we integrate
this service with Pub/Sub

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:38.260
so our order topic
can send messages

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.300
to the packaging subscriber?

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.230
Well, let's see.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:45.820
We go to IAM and enable Pub/Sub
to create authentication tokens

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.040
in our project.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.380
We locate the Cloud Pub/Sub
service account and add a role,

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.260
service account
token creator, to it.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.880
Then we create the
service account

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.090
to represent the Pub/Sub
subscription identity.

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:00.640
For that, we go to
service accounts in IAM

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.930
and create a new
service account.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.780
Now, we will give
the invoker service

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:07.750
the account permissions it
needs to invoke our packaging

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:08.740
service.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:11.290
For this, we use
gcloud run service,

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:15.130
add IAM policy binding, the name
of our service, which we called

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.700
packaging-service, the member,
which is our service account,

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.800
and the role is invoker.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.960
Now we create the
Pub/Sub subscription

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:26.140
for our packaging service
with this service account.

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:30.160
We execute the command gcloud
pubsub subscriptions create,

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.650
provide the name of the
subscription, topic that we

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:34.540
want to be associated with.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.560
In our case, it's order_topic.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:40.540
The push endpoint needs to
be our Cloud Run service URL,

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:43.600
and the push auth
service account flag

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.030
activates the Pub/Sub
push functionality

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.670
for authentication
and authorization.

00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:52.270
We go to Pub/Sub and see that
our subscription is created.

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:54.310
With that, Cat-Out's
packaging service

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.650
is fully integrated
with Pub/Sub,

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:00.160
and we are ready to send a
message from order publisher

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.050
to our packaging subscriber.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:05.530
And to verify that our Cloud Run
packaging subscriber received

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.750
it, we go to the
logs in Stackdriver

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.430
and filter on Cloud Run.

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.050
And we see that the
message is received.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:13.360
So there you have it.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.340
We just set up an integration
between Cloud Pub/Sub and Cloud

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:16.840
Run.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.180
All right, so today
we helped Cat-Out

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:21.940
deploy a push
subscriber on Cloud Run

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.680
and receive messages
from the older

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.630
service using Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.280
Stay tuned for the
next episode, where

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.370
we will help Cat-Out
replay and purge messages.

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:32.290
Until then, give us
a like, subscribe,

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.440
and let us know what you want
to learn about Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:39.090
[INSTRUMENTAL MUSIC]

